First, restart your Postman application to clear any temporary issues. Then, make sure you have saved your current work in the tests within Postman. You can re-run the tests for the new API endpoint using the following command in your terminal:

```bash
npm test src/api/tests/api.test.js
```

Make sure to check the error handling in `src/errors/Handler.js` to ensure it catches and reports errors correctly. If the tests pass, you can confidently push your changes. Here’s an example of a test you might want to include:

```javascript
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../index');

describe('POST /api/v1/resource', () => {
  it('should create a new resource', async () => {
    const response = await request(app)
      .post('/api/v1/resource')
      .send({ name: 'New Resource' });
    expect(response.statusCode).toBe(201);
  });
});
```

Make sure to re-run your Jest tests to validate everything is working as expected.
